 festival may have been the  absolute peak of 2010's  scammer culture and fire fraud tells the  full story in  excruciating detail in case you didn't  know this 2017 festival  promised to be one of the most exclusive  parties of the year  promotional material depicted the event  as one absolutely  dripping in luxury but when the day of  the festival actually arrived  those promises failed to materialize now  total chaos has erupted  fans are fleeing the fire festival on  emergency flights  out of the bahamas by focusing on billy  mcfarland the entrepreneur at the center  of the fire festival plan  fire fraud really gets to the rot at the  heart of the event  specifically it asks how he managed to  convince the world that he knew what he  was doing  even when he really really didn't the  documentary spends plenty of time on the  build up to the festival  but it's definitely not time wasted as  the payoff is almost  ludicrously sweet the fire festival  scandal was broadly publicized  as it happened but the documentary  exposes the event's horrible conditions  in even closer detail  fire fraud also gets at something deeper  about the culture mcfarland exploited  by suggesting that people are most  easily fooled when they're offered  something too glamorous to pass up  when you think of kristen stewart you  probably don't think of quirky rom-coms  but if happiest season is  any indication maybe you want to this  charming movie stars stewart is abby and  mackenzie davis as harper a couple who  journey to harper's family home for  christmas  there's just one problem harper's  straight-laced parents don't know that  their darling daughter is a lesbian  as a result abby has to pretend that  they're just close friends  even though she's been eagerly planning  to pop the question on christmas morning  happiest season plays into a number of  tropes found in the romantic comedy  genre  this doesn't drag the film down however  in fact its strong central performances  breathe  new life into those old cliches this  movie  is a sweet and delightful festive  diversion  and serves a reminder that good actors  can do great work  in any genre pete davidson's turn toward  the dramatic may seem like a surprise  but for the most part  the public seems to have embraced it he  got a whole heap of positive attention  for the king of staten island  and has now earned further praise for a  supporting turn in big time adolescence  this movie follows davidson's zeke a  drug-dealing college dropout who becomes  a mentor to a rule-abiding team  this is a classic coming-of-age story  and certainly features a great  deal of the genre's typical conventions  thanks to a sharp script  and a precise ear for dialogue however  big time adolescence  manages to transcend its formula  davidson  in particular earned praise for his  performance in the film which is a much  more sensitive term than what people  have come to expect from him  the entire cast shines though and  they're a huge part of what makes big  time adolescence one of hulu's best  films untouchable was one of the first  documentaries to tackle the rise and  fall of harvey weinstein  although the technical filmmaking behind  the movie is imperfect  untouchable nonetheless earned praise  from critics in part because of the  urgency of the story that it tells the  movie was released in 2018  a year after bombshell accounts of  weinstein's history of abuse began to be  published across  major media platforms sparking the  hashtag metoo movement  viewers who know those stories may not  learn anything new and untouchable but  this doc  does at least bring them together in a  much wider context  as has become clear in the years since  the weinstein story first broke  the now reviled producer was far from  the only hollywood personality to get  away with horrendous abuses of power  suitably then untouchable examines not  just the allegations against weinstein  himself  but also the systems that enabled him to  hold on to his power for so long  the stories following the creation of  comic book characters are often stories  of credit  about those who get it for bringing such  iconic characters to life  and those who are ultimately brushed to  the side in batman and bill  audiences are treated to the story of  bill finger an unheralded comic book  writer who played a crucial role in  creating batman  it wasn't until after finger's death  that people began to recognize the  massive contributions he made to the  character  but this film asks why bob kane instead  got all the credit for creating batman  even though finger contributed the  character's signature look  his detective skills and many of his  arch nemesis  among other crucial details they would  hire ghost writers back in the day  bob came to get credit for the story  he'd hand it in here's my batman story  like bob york genius and you know that's  how the industry worked for a long long  time  sadly it took years for fingers legacy  to be fully restored but now  at least the world knows that finger  played just as important to role as kane  if not  more so and this movie is one of the  reasons why  run is a taut sleek well-acted  thriller starring sarah paulson as a  highly protective mother and kiera allen  as her sick daughter  the movie takes pains to establish their  close relationship  before things take several increasingly  dark turns  at its heart run is a movie about being  imprisoned without even knowing it  it's also about realizing the person  you've spent your whole life with  isn't who they say they are but while  these thematic elements might feel  weighty the movie itself is surprisingly  light on its feet  thanks to expert work from both paulson  and alan  these broadly drawn characters feel  distinctly human  and even as the circumstances of run  become increasingly absurd  the relationship between the characters  keeps the story grounded with genuine  emotional stakes  lin-manuel miranda is one of the most  prolific creators working today  thanks in part to the success of  hamilton he now has his hands on a vast  array of different projects  often acting producing writing directing  or doing  any combination of those things in we  are freestyle love supreme  miranda helps to tell the story of a  group he started in 2004  years before anyone knew who he was the  group was dedicated to freestyle rap  and this documentary directed by andrew  fried tells their story while paying  tribute to the art form in which they  specialized  obviously miranda is an important part  of this story but he's not  the sum total and even those who might  be worn out by his peppy personality  will find  plenty to love in we are freestyle love  supreme after all  freestyle rap is about creating  something beautiful in real time  and the scenes here that highlight that  sense of spontaneity make the  documentary more than worth watch  although it shares a title with the  famous dostoyevsky novel crime and  punishment is actually a documentary  firmly rooted in the american experience  the film follows latino and black police  officers in the nypd over the course of  four years  chronicling their brush ups against the  system and the ways in which policing is  biased toward people in their  communities  crime and punishment doesn't come to any  easy answers about how the nypd can be  fixed  instead it simply shines a light on the  everyday corruption that's become a part  of policing  and the other systemic abuses every  police officer eventually has to deal  with  nor is crime and punishment a loud  shouting movie instead  it makes its points quietly and  precisely ultimately though  that only reinforces the movie's power  making it all the more clear that  problems endemic to policing  aren't going anywhere it's an urgent  vital movie that gets at the humanity of  the police officers themselves  even as it highlights the corruption of  the institution in which they operate  unlike many of the documentaries on hulu  the subject matter and  vibe of ask dr ruth is pretty easy going  the film chronicles the life of dr ruth  westheimer a sex therapist who  catapulted to fame late in her life  thanks to a tv show in which she  provided candid advice to callers about  their sex lives  what makes dr ruth remarkable is her  utter willingness to discuss sex in a  way  most americans do not she's not shy at  all  and her frankness about sex has arguably  helped spur a new sense of openness  towards the subject  thanks to her slight stature old age and  charming accent  dr ruth has also been able to reach a  whole lot of people who may have  otherwise  tuned her out ask dr ruth as a deeply  charming film but it's also one that  encourages open conversation about  topics that might otherwise be seen as  taboo  according to dr ruth herself sex should  not be a topic we're afraid of  we should be willing to dive in head  first she says no matter what may come  of it  and this documentary embodies that  stance with open-hearted abandon  palm springs debuted fairly early on in  the american covit 19 lockdown  and it couldn't have been more perfectly  timed  the comedy which stars andy samberg and  kristen miliotti follows milioti's  character as she becomes embroiled in a  time loop that sandberg's character has  been stuck in for some time  it's one of those infinite time loop  situations you might heard about  that i might have heard about yeah the  movie has plenty of fun without conceit  but it also updates the groundhog day  formula by asking what would happen to a  relationship  in which both people are forced to  relive the same day over and over again  thanks to note perfect performances from  sandberg and milioti  plus a little help from jk simmons palm  springs is  always charming and frequently moving as  one of the very best  movies that hulu has to offer it  delivers not just laughs  but genuine emotion and a beautiful love  story to boot  with the advent of influencer culture  the world has been treated to an  entirely new type of celebrity and for  some young people the possibilities that  the influencer lifestyle offers is  tantalizing  jawline follows austin tester a teenager  from tennessee trying to make it big  online so he can escape his small town  life  although there are plenty of movies out  there about the horrors of the internet  jawline is not one of them instead it's  a tender look at one kid who's trying to  make it  and a subculture that has a tendency to  consume people like him  and spit them out without ever  acknowledging their humanity  jawline doesn't pass any judgment on its  subject instead arguing that the idea of  internet fame  is so attractive because it offers a  route out of the dead end  something that seems increasingly hard  to find in today's world  sometimes documentaries choose to tell  stories that most people  are already pretty familiar with other  times though they introduce  brand new subjects to a largely ignorant  audience  so it goes in dumb the story of big  brother magazine  director patrick o'dell shines the  spotlight on this obscure publication  which  somehow catapulted a generation of  skaters to fame  and also led to the creation of mtv's  jackass featuring interviews with a  number of the era's icons  this movie tells the story of a  generation of young rebels who  eventually became incredibly successful  within their fields  dumb isn't quite as widely known as many  of the films on this list  but it's earned plenty of praise from  critics who hailed the film as an  effective chronicle of a group of people  who felt like breaking the rules  for no other reason than because they  could  although it wasn't as much of a  phenomenon as the original march of the  penguins  this sequel features all of the elements  that make the first film so winning  morgan freeman returns to narrate the  project and the story at the film center  is just as compelling as anything that  happens in the first film  the next step follows a group of  penguins as they journey to a mysterious  destination  after one of their young hears an  enigmatic call above all else  this sequel which came more than a  decade after the original film  serves as proof that penguins make  deeply compelling subjects for nature  documentaries  of course even as the documentary  wanders at their beauty  it's hard not to see the impending  threat of climate change hanging over  their heads  to that end march of the penguins 2  isn't just about the beauty of antarctic  wildlife  it's also a harsh reminder that it may  not be around forever  too funny to fail is both hilarious and  refreshingly low stakes  the documentary tells the story of the  dana carvey show specifically all the  ways in which it failed to succeed  despite the many talented people working  behind the scenes  stephen colbert steve carell and of  course dana carvey  all worked on the show but that didn't  keep it from flaming out in truly  stupendous fashion  in spite of the show's untimely demise  this documentary remains a fairly  lighthearted look at what went wrong  including candid interviews with all of  these now famous men  as it turns out the dana carvey show may  have been ahead of its time and  too funny to fail demonstrates how well  many of the show's sketches still hold  up  and although its failure might have been  kind of depressing at the time  the story here is told with a wry smile  and the ever useful benefit of hindsight  most magic shows tend to opt for  spectacle over meaning  within and of itself however magician  derek delgadio steers hard into the  world of storytelling  mixing amazing tricks with his own  personal narrative the show becomes an  experience that's both  dazzling and deeply moving in and of  itself is a filmed version of delgado's  stage show of the same name  but it beautifully translates the  experience to screen delgado performs  several tricks over the course of the  show and none of them lose any of their  intense impact through the process of  being filmed but though the tricks may  be astounding they're also pretty deep  delgado show is an examination of how we  see ourselves  and how that self perception helps to  guide the decisions we make  it's absolutely mesmerizing and arguably  one of the best things on hulu full stop  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  your favorite movies are coming soon  subscribe to our youtube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one
 

